+# Daily Grind security audit and rollout
+
+## Inventory and findings
+
+The audited application is a static HTML document served by a small Node HTTP
+server. Every existing document route falls back to this page. There are no
+client scripts, DOM sinks, inline event handlers, forms, cookies, authentication,
+window messaging, OAuth popups, embeds, or privileged browser APIs. `/logout` is
+a placeholder response, not a server-side session revocation implementation.
+
+Previously, responses had no security headers. The document had an inline style
+block and a style attribute, both incompatible with a strict stylesheet policy.
+These styles now live in `/styles.css`, preserving the existing presentation.
+The only third-party dependency remains the CSS hero image at
+`https://images.unsplash.com`. There are no third-party scripts or stylesheets
+requiring SRI. The unversioned image is explicitly allowed; no COEP policy is
+enabled because this site does not require cross-origin isolation.
+
+## Implemented protections
+
+- CSP defaults to blocking resources. It allows same-origin stylesheets and
+  Unsplash images, blocks all scripts (including event handlers), connections,
+  forms, frames, workers, plugins, and base-tag overrides, and restricts framing
+  to the same origin. No unsafe-inline, unsafe-eval, nonce, or script hash is
+  needed because the site has no authorized scripts.
+- Trusted Types blocks raw-string writes to script/HTML sinks in supporting
+  browsers and disallows policy creation. There is no pass-through policy or
+  client polyfill; the site works without JavaScript in older browsers too.
+- All responses, including errors and logout, receive MIME sniffing, referrer,
+  framing, browser-feature, opener, resource-isolation, and no-store headers.
+  COOP uses `same-origin-allow-popups` rather than the stricter `same-origin`.
+- Fetch Metadata denies cross-origin resource requests, including those from
+  sibling subdomains, while permitting ordinary document navigation. Cross-site
+  logout navigation is denied. Clients without Fetch Metadata continue to work;
+  these checks supplement CSP and are not authentication or CSRF tokens.
+- Logout sends `Clear-Site-Data` for cookies, storage, and cache. Future real
+  authentication must also revoke the server session and use a CSRF-protected
+  state-changing endpoint and Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite cookies.
+- The reporting collector accepts legacy CSP reports and Reporting API batches.
+  It limits bodies to 16 KiB, batches to 20 entries, and accepted submissions to
+  30 per minute per process. Logs contain only allowlisted effective directives
+  and disposition, never URLs, source code, user agents, or raw reports. Reports
+  are untrusted telemetry and may be forged. This is categorical telemetry, not
+  a full diagnostic archive. Keep HTTP/proxy access logs from capturing report
+  bodies, credentials, and sensitive URL queries as well.
+
+## Deployment and future changes
+
+1. Serve production through HTTPS at the trusted reverse proxy. Set
+   `PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://your-production-host` to the site's actual origin,
+   without a path or credentials. The server deliberately ignores request Host
+   and forwarded headers when constructing reporting endpoints. Without this
+   configuration, legacy `report-uri /security-reports` still works, but modern
+   `Reporting-Endpoints` and HSTS are omitted. Modern Reporting API endpoints
+   require absolute URLs ([Reporting API documentation](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/capabilities/web-apis/reporting-api)).
+2. Preserve the Node response headers at the proxy/CDN and allow POST requests
+   to `/security-reports`. Route every document and `/styles.css` through this
+   handler, or copy equivalent headers into any separate static hosting layer.
+   Browser security policies must be HTTP response headers: a meta tag cannot
+   supply report-only policies or framing protection. If hosting assets on a
+   different origin, review both CSP and CORP first.
+3. Enforcement is the default for the audited script-free site. Before adding
+   third-party resources, scripts, payments, or other integrations, stage the
+   intended policy with `SECURITY_POLICY_MODE=report-only`. This intentionally
+   stops enforcing the resource/Trusted Types restrictions, while retaining
+   framing, plugin, base-tag and companion protections. Monitor categorized
+   CSP reports across representative traffic for days to weeks, distinguish
+   extension noise from actual failures, resolve dependencies and sinks, then
+   restore `SECURITY_POLICY_MODE=enforce`. Report-only mode is a temporary
+   migration setting, not equivalent protection. Invalid mode values fail startup.
+4. HTTPS deployments initially send `Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=300`.
+   After validating HTTPS across production, increase `HSTS_MAX_AGE` gradually,
+   up to `31536000`. `HSTS_MAX_AGE=0` expires HSTS learned from this host. No
+   subdomain or preload commitment is made automatically. TLS termination and
+   production header delivery still need deployment-level validation.
+5. Send the sanitized JSON report log events to the existing operations log
+   collector and watch for new directive/disposition combinations after releases.
+   Avoid widening script origin allowlists to resolve violations: if executable
+   code becomes necessary, audit it and use explicit hashes or per-response
+   nonces. Avoid adding a permissive Trusted Types default policy.
+
+## Verification scope
+
+Run `npm test` and `npm run build`. Tests invoke the standalone request handler
+with mocked requests/responses and streams; they do not import `server.js`,
+start a server, or launch a browser. They cover policy/markup compatibility,
+response MIME types, security headers, HTTPS configuration, report-only mode,
+Fetch Metadata routing, logout/HEAD behavior, report privacy, malformed input,
+body/batch/rate limits, and Host-header isolation. The build uses syntax checks
+only. No browser behavior, real traffic, TLS, or deployed CDN headers have been
+verified as part of this change.
